
{"id":"4031393f-3288-4859-a447-a2be04809e48","object":"chat.completion","created":1784983124,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-CR-02. The payload contains \"items\" (list of strings), \"required_failure_items\" (list of strings), and \"_case_nonce\" (string). The problem is described: \"Apply deterministic chunk-removal and granularity-refinement passes while preserving a supplied syntax and interestingness oracle.\" There's mention of \"chunk_reduction_schedule\", \"granularity\", \"chunks\", \"accept removal only if required failure items remain\", \"restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\", \"double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity\", \"return reduced items, attempt count, and explicit one-minimal check\".\n\nWe need to simulate a reduction process. The algorithm: start at granularity 2. Split items into chunks of that size. Try removing each chunk (left to right) and check if the resulting list still contains all required_failure_items. If removal keeps required items, accept it (remove that chunk) and then restart from the beginning with slightly coarser granularity (maybe increase by 1?). The description: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\"\n\nInterpretation: granularity is the size of chunks. Start at 2. For each level, we try to remove chunks in order. If a removal succeeds (i.e., after removal, all required_failure_items are still in the list), we remove that chunk, then we increase granularity by a small amount? \"Slightly coarser\" might mean increase by 1? But then \"double granularity when no removal succeeds\". Possibly: after each successful removal, increase granularity by 1 (coarser). When no removal succeeds at a given granularity, double the granularity (if still less than singleton?). \"Stop at singleton granularity\" means we stop when granularity reaches 1? But we double, so could overshoot. Perhaps we stop when granularity > len(items) after doubling? Or we stop when we cannot double further? The phrasing: \"and stop at singleton granularity\" suggests that the process ends when granularity becomes 1 (i.e., each chunk is a single item). But we start at 2, and if no removal succeeds, we double granularity. If we double from 2 -> 4 -> 8 -> ... until we reach a size where chunks are size 1? That would be when granularity >= len(items)? But they say \"singleton granularity\" meaning each item is its own chunk. That would be granularity 1. But we are starting at 2, and only double when no removal succeeds. So we might never reach 1 if we only double? Unless we also have a mechanism to decrease granularity after a successful removal? The description says \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That implies after a removal, we increase granularity a little (maybe +1). Then if we fail at that new granularity, we double? But then after doubling, we are even coarser, and might still fail. Eventually we might reach a point where granularity is so coarse that removing a chunk would remove many items and likely fail to keep required items. But we need to stop at singleton granularity, meaning we eventually reduce granularity back to 1? The description is ambiguous.\n\nMaybe the intended algorithm is more like \"delta debugging\" but with chunk sizes. Actually, the description: \"Apply deterministic chunk-removal and granularity-refinement passes while preserving a supplied syntax and interestingness oracle.\" There's reference to \"chunk_reduction_schedule\". Possibly a known algorithm? Could be similar to \"Perses\" or \"Hdd\" (hierarchical delta debugging). But we need to implement it based on the bounded objective.\n\nGiven the fixture payloads, we have items list of [t0..t9], and required_failure_items list of 2 items. We can test our algorithm.\n\nLet's parse the algorithm more carefully:\n\n- Start chunk removal at granularity two.\n- Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain.\n- Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\n- Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\n\nInterpretation: The process loops over levels of granularity. At each level, we attempt to remove chunks. If any chunk removal is successful, we restart the level with a coarser granularity (increase by 1? \"slightly coarser\" could mean +1). If no chunk removal succeeds at current granularity, we double the granularity and continue. We stop when granularity reaches 1 (singleton) or perhaps when no further reductions are possible.\n\nBut also \"stop at singleton granularity\" suggests that we finally try granularity 1 (each item individually) and if no removal succeeds at that level, we stop. But starting at 2, if we double when failing, we go to 4,8,16,... we might skip 1. So perhaps the process is: start at 2, then after a successful removal, we increase granularity by 1 (coarser) until we reach a level where no removal succeeds, then we double? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity\" could mean: if no removal succeeds at current granularity, we double the granularity. If after doubling, the granularity becomes greater than the length of the current items? But \"singleton granularity\" might mean that we stop when the granularity is such that each chunk is a single element (i.e., granularity=1). But doubling from 2 gives 4, from 4 gives 8, etc. Only if we start at 1 could we double to 2, etc. Perhaps the process starts at granularity 2, but after a successful removal, we decrease granularity? The phrase \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" is ambiguous: \"slightly coarser\" could mean larger chunks (i.e., increase granularity). That seems counterintuitive: after a successful removal, we want to try larger chunks? That might be to see if we can remove even larger chunks. Then if we fail to remove any chunk at that larger size, we double (making even larger). That seems like we are always increasing granularity. But then we stop when we reach a size where chunks are of size 1? But 1 is smaller, not larger. So maybe \"coarser\" means smaller chunks? In many reduction algorithms, \"coarser\" means larger granularity? Actually, \"coarser\" granularity means larger pieces. \"Finer\" means smaller. So \"slightly coarser\" means increase chunk size. So after a successful removal, we try larger chunks to see if we can remove even more. If no removal succeeds, we double the chunk size (even larger). Eventually we reach a chunk size that is larger than the list length, which makes no sense. So maybe we need to decrease granularity after a successful removal? That is more typical: remove small chunks, then refine to smaller chunks to try to remove sub-parts.\n\nLet's think about the problem context: it's about \"chunk reduction schedule\" and \"granularity refinement\". Possibly it's like the \"bisecting\" or \"delta debug\" algorithm. The standard delta debugging (ddmin) starts with a set of changes, splits into subsets, tests, etc. But here we have items and required_failure_items. It's about removing items while preserving the property that the required failure items remain. So it's a minimization problem: find a minimal subset of items that still contains all required_failure_items. The algorithm described seems like a greedy approach with chunk removal.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll look at the expected outputs from the fixture? The fixture shows only three cases, but we need to implement the function that returns attempts, one_minimal, reduced_items. We don't have expected outputs, but we can infer from the description.\n\nPerhaps the intended algorithm is:\n\n- Start with granularity g = 2.\n- While g <= len(items):\n   - For each chunk of size g (non-overlapping, left to right), try to remove that chunk. If after removal, the set of required_failure_items is still a subset of the remaining items, accept removal and break out of the loop.\n   - If a removal was accepted, set g = g + 1 (slightly coarser) and continue (restart with new items and new g).\n   - If no removal accepted, set g = g * 2 (double), and continue.\n   - Stop when g > len(items) (or g becomes 1? but we start at 2, doubling goes 2,4,8,... so we may exceed length). But \"stop at singleton granularity\" might mean we also try g=1 at the end? Or maybe we start at 2, and after doubling, we eventually get to a g that is 1? That can't happen.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"granularity\" means the number of chunks? Or the chunk size? Usually granularity refers to chunk size. \"Singleton granularity\" means each item is a single chunk, i.e., chunk size 1.\n\nPerhaps the algorithm is:\n\n- Start with granularity = len(items) // 2? No.\n\nLet's search memory: There is a known algorithm \"Chunk Reduction\" used in test case reduction, such as in \"Delta Debugging\" or \"Perses\". Often they use a hierarchy: start with large chunks, split into halves, etc. But here it says start at granularity two. That is small.\n\nMaybe we need to simulate the algorithm as described step by step, ignoring the \"coarser\" confusion. Let's assume:\n\n- Let current_items = list of items.\n- Let g = 2.\n- While g <= len(current_items):\n    - For i from 0 to len(current_items)-g+1 step g (non-overlapping chunks):\n        - Suppose we remove items[i:i+g] from current_items.\n        - If all required_failure_items are in the resulting list, then:\n            - Remove that chunk (set current_items = new list).\n            - Set g = g