hat would be the same as g=1. So we need to include g=1 in the progression. Perhaps the algorithm is: start with g=2, try removals. If success, increase g by 1. If fail, decrease g by dividing by 2? But text says \"double\". Alternatively, maybe \"double granularity\" means the number of chunks doubles? That would be the same as halving the chunk size. For example, \"granularity\" might be the number of chunks, not chunk size. If granularity is number of chunks, then higher granularity means more chunks (finer). Starting at 2 chunks. Then \"slightly coarser\" would mean fewer chunks (increase chunk size). \"Double granularity\" would mean more chunks (finer). That might make sense: Start with 2 chunks (split into two halves). Try removing each half. If removal succeeds, restart with coarser granularity (fewer chunks, i.e., larger chunks). If no removal succeeds, double the number of chunks (finer granularity). Stop when granularity reaches the number of items (singleton, each item is its own chunk). That aligns: double granularity = increase number of chunks, making chunks smaller. \"Slightly coarser\" after successful removal would mean reduce number of chunks, making chunks larger. The progression: start with 2 chunks. If success, go to 1 chunk? But 1 chunk is the whole list; removing the whole list would lose required items, so unlikely. \"Slightly coarser\" after success: maybe reduce to 1 chunk? But then stop? Alternatively, after success, we might set granularity = 1 (single chunk) but then try to remove that chunk (but that would lose all items, so not allowed). That seems degenerate. Or maybe after success we set granularity = granularity - 1? If starting at 2, after success, set to 1. Then we try to remove that single chunk? That would remove everything, which fails. Then we double to 2 again? That would loop. Not great.\n\nLet's consider the chunk size interpretation but with adding a final pass for g=1.\n\nI'll simulate the algorithm for case 0 with g starting at 2, g increments on success, doubles on failure, and when g exceeds length, we set g=1 and do one more pass? That could produce a reasonable result.\n\nFor case 0: items = [t0..t9], required = [t0,t9].\n- g=2: chunks: [t0,t1], [t2,t3], [t4,t5], [t6,t7], [t8,t9]. Check each removal: \n   Remove [t0,t1] -> remaining [t2..t9] contains t9 but not t0? t0 is missing, so fail. Remove [t2,t3] -> remaining [t0,t1,t4..t9] contains both t0 and t9? t0 still there, t9 there, so success. Accept removal of [t2,t3]? But the description says \"accept removal only if required failure items remain.\" So yes. After removal, items become [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9]. Now restart with slightly coarser granularity. \"slightly coarser\" could be g = g+1 = 3 (since we were at 2, now 3). But we could also interpret as restart the process from the beginning (meaning g=2 again?) The phrase \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" suggests that after removal, we do not reset g to 2, but increase it. So g becomes 3. Now items length=8.\n- g=3: chunks: [t0,t1,t4], [t5,t6,t7], [t8,t9] (last chunk size 2). Try removing first chunk [t0,t1,t4]: remaining = [t5,t6,t7,t8,t9]; contains t9 but not t0, fails. Remove second chunk [t5,t6,t7]: remaining = [t0,t1,t4,t8,t9]; contains t0 and t9? t0 present, t9 present, success. Accept removal. Items become [t0,t1,t4,t8,t9]. Length=5. Now increase g to 4.\n- g=4: chunks: [t0,t1,t4,t8], [t9] (last chunk size 1). Remove first: remaining [t9] lacks t0, fail. Remove second: remaining [t0,t1,t4,t8] lacks t9, fail. No success. So double granularity: g=8.\n- g=8: chunks: [t0,t1,t4,t8,t9] (only one chunk of size 5, but g=8 > len=5, so chunks? Actually chunk size 8, but only one chunk covering all 5 items). Remove that chunk -> remaining [] lacks both, fail. No success. Double again to g=16 > len. Now we have no chunk to try? Or we might consider removing the whole list? That would fail. Then we should try g=1. So set g=1.\n- g=1: chunks: each item individually. For each item, try removal: remove t0 -> remaining [t1,t4,t8,t9] lacks t0, fail. remove t1 -> remaining [t0,t4,t8,t9] contains t0,t9? t0 present, t9 present, success? t1 is not required, so removing it keeps required items. So accept removal. Items become [t0,t4,t8,t9]. Then restart with slightly coarser granularity: g=2 (since g=1+1=2). But we already had g=1, and after successful removal, we increase to 2. Now items length=4.\n- g=2: chunks: [t0,t4], [t8,t9]. Remove first: remaining [t8,t9] lacks t0, fail. Remove second: remaining [t0,t4] lacks t9, fail. No success. Double g=4.\n- g=4: chunks: [t0,t4,t8,t9] (one chunk). Remove -> empty, fail. No success. Double g=8 > len, then try g=1.\n- g=1: chunks: t0, t4, t8, t9. Try remove t0 fail, remove t4 -> remaining [t0,t8,t9] contains t0,t9, success. Accept removal. Items become [t0,t8,t9]. Increase g=2.\n- g=2: chunks: [t0,t8], [t9]. Remove first: remaining [t9] lacks t0, fail. Remove second: remaining [t0,t8] lacks t9, fail. No success. Double g=4 > len, then g=1.\n- g=1: chunks: t0, t8, t9. Try remove t0 fail, remove t8 -> remaining [t0,t9] contains both, success. Accept removal. Items become [t0,t9]. Increase g=2.\n- g=2: chunks: [t0,t9] (one chunk). Remove -> empty, fail. No success. Double g=4 > len, then g=1.\n- g=1: chunks: t0, t9. Try remove t0 fail, remove t9 fail. No success. Double g=2? but we have special condition? Stop at singleton granularity? At g=1, we have tried all, no removal, and we are at singleton, so stop.\n\nFinal items: [t0,t9]. Attempts: we need to count all checks. Let's count: \nFor case 0: with initial list 10, we had attempts: \n- g=2: 5 chunks -> 5 attempts (plus maybe each chunk check? Yes. But we only count when we actually evaluate the condition? In our simulation we do condition check for each chunk. So that's 5 attempts for first round).\n- Then after success, g=3: 3 chunks -> 3 attempts.\n- g=4: 2 chunks -> 2 attempts.\n- g=8: 1 chunk -> 1 attempt.\n- Then g=1: 5 chunks -> 5 attempts.\n- Then g=2: 2 chunks -> 2 attempts.\n- g=4: 1 chunk -> 1 attempt.\n- g=1: 4 chunks -> 4 attempts.\n- g=2: 2 chunks -> 2 attempts.\n- g=4: 1 chunk -> 1 attempt.\n- g=1: 3 chunks -> 3 attempts.\n- g=2: 1 chunk -> 1 attempt.\n- g=4: 1 chunk -> 1 attempt.\n- g=1: 2 chunks -> 2 attempts.\nTotal attempts = 5+3+2+1+5+2+1+4+2+1+3+1+1+2 = 33? But this seems high. Might be correct.\n\nBut the algorithm I've described is arbitrary, especially the \"when g > len, set g=1\". The description says \"stop at singleton granularity\", maybe meaning that the algorithm will eventually try g=1 and then stop regardless of success. In my simulation, after the last g=1 with no success, we stop. That seems plausible.\n\nBut is there a simpler interpretation? Perhaps the algorithm only tries each granularity once (i.e., doesn't restart after success). But it says \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" So it does restart.\n\nGiven the complexity, maybe the expected algorithm is different. Let's look at the problem statement: \"Apply deterministic chunk-removal and granularity-refinement passes while preserving a supplied syntax and interestingness oracle.\" This sounds like a typical reduction algorithm used in program reduction tools (e.g., Perses). The \"granularity\" might refer to the size of the token tree nodes being removed. But here we are given a list of items.\n\nAnother possibility: The algorithm is exactly the one described in the paper \"Perses: Syntax-Guided Program Reduction\" where they use a priority queue of nodes and iteratively remove them. But the description here is simpler.\n\nGiven the time, I think we need to produce a Python implementation that follows the literal description as closely as possible, even if the interpretation is ambiguous. The important thing is that the function returns a JSON object with attempts, one_minimal, reduced_items. We'll implement a deterministic algorithm that matches the steps: start g=2, try chunks (non-overlapping, continuous). On success, remove chunk, set g = g + 1 (or maybe g = current chunk index? \"slightly coarser\" could mean increase by 1). On no success, double g. When g becomes greater than current length, we set g=1 (singleton) and try. When at g=1 and no success, we stop. Additionally, we need to avoid infinite loops by ensuring we don't repeat the same state.\n\nWe'll also compute one_minimal: after reduction, check for each item if removal causes required_failure_items to be missing. If any item can be removed while keeping required items, one_minimal is False; else True. But the algorithm might not guarantee one-minimality. However, the output requires an explicit check.\n\nAttempts count: count each time we check the condition for a potential removal.\n\nLet's write the code accordingly. We'll implement solve(case) that takes payload (dict) and returns dict with keys attempts, one_minimal, reduced_items.\n\nBe careful: case has items (list of strings), required_failure_items (list of strings), _case_nonce (string, ignored). We'll ignore nonce.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Extract items and required items. Ensure required items are a set for quick membership.\n\n2. Initialize:\n   - current_items = list(items) (do not mutate input)\n   - attempts = 0\n   - granularity = 2\n   - We'll loop indefinitely, with a max iteration guard to avoid infinite loops (e.g., 1000).\n\n3. Loop:\n   - If granularity > len(current_items):\n        - If granularity == 1? Actually we need to handle. Based on \"stop at singleton granularity\", we might want to set granularity = 1 if we are currently > length and haven't tried 1. So we can have a boolean tried_singleton = False. When granularity > len, and not tried_singleton, set granularity = 1. Then after processing g=1, if no success, we break.\n   - Else if granularity == 1: we are at singleton, process, then break after regardless of success? The description says \"stop at singl